        "/tmp/101-ios-refusal-old-regression/src/scanner-session.test.ts": "54e305afad6afeb7fdd8137aa482be7765d6ab0c4659a504c19a76c017d8675e"
      },
      "assessment": {
        "currentOwnership": "The new setError executes synchronously only after scanner.begin confirms a mounted foreground attempt and before the permission await. There is no yield between generation acquisition and the write. Permission resolution/rejection still checks scanner.current(expected, active) before any later feedback or camera state change.",
        "replacementAndNavigation": "A replacement begin retires the old generation. Manual-entry navigation retires the current generation and clears error; both old and replacement permission completions remain stale and cannot restore guidance or mount a camera. The added captured-completion regression exercises this ordering.",
        "backgroundInactiveUnmount": "Inactive begin returns before the new write. Background and unmount retire the pending generation; a later permission result cannot alter camera state or feedback. Guidance already published by the foreground attempt may remain after retirement, but it is purpose/manual-recovery text and does not claim that denial occurred.",
        "cancelRequestCloseAcceptedScan": "Existing actual-handler regressions still fence queued camera callbacks on Cancel, request-close, navigation, background, unmount and accepted scan. The delta does not weaken these generation checks.",
        "truthfulness": "\u201c101 Link needs the camera only to read the pairing code. You can paste the code instead.\u201d truthfully states the purpose and equivalent recovery before requesting permission. It does not state that permission was denied or that an AppState transition occurred.",
        "testRealism": "The VM fixture executes the actual TSX handlers and the real scanner-session module while stubbing React state and the unavailable Expo permission bridge. It proves callback/feedback ownership only."
      },
